<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/Developer2.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 me-3 p-3" alt="Quality Management" /></div>
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/woman-tech-desk-chart-bureau.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" alt="Quality Management" /></div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-8 Imgborder" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Quality Management Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>
                <p>Manufacturers may struggle with manual inspections, inconsistent quality records, delayed issue resolution, limited traceability, and fragmented quality information.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Manual Quality Checks: Digitize inspection and quality workflows to improve consistency.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Delayed Issue Resolution: Automate alerts, escalation, and corrective-action workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Inconsistent Quality Records: Centralize quality information using structured data and standardized workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Limited Traceability: Connect quality records with production, materials, suppliers, and relevant operational information.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Limited Quality Reporting: Create dashboards and reports for defects, inspections, corrective actions, and performance.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>The Importance of Customization in Quality Management</b></h3>
                <p>Manufacturers have different inspection procedures, quality standards, products, processes, and corrective-action workflows. Customization helps the solution support these requirements.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Customize Inspection Workflows: Configure quality checks according to products, processes, materials, and operations.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Create Role-Based Workspaces: Provide quality, production, procurement, and management teams with relevant actions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Automate Corrective Actions: Configure alerts, approvals, escalation, and follow-up workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Build Quality Dashboards: Provide insights into defects, inspections, trends, corrective actions, and quality performance.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Gather User Feedback: Continuously improve quality processes based on operational feedback.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

